SIMP_END=[eq(r'S_4=\frac{0.75}3[1+4(0.0625+1.5625)+2(0.25)+4]'),eq(r'=0.2(1+6.5+0.50+4)=3'),paragraph('The exact integral is:'),eq(r'I=\int_{-1}^2x^2\,dx=\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_{-1}^2=\frac83+\frac13=3'),paragraph('The error term is:'),eq(r'E_2=I-S_4=3-3=0'),paragraph('This develops the idea that Simpson’s rule is much more accurate than trapezoidal rule.'),note('The decimal multiplier 0.2 is a typo: 0.75/3=0.25, and the bracket is 12, giving 3. Here the rule is exact because x² is a polynomial of degree at most 3. The source E₂ counts two panels while S₄ counts four subintervals; either label convention must be explained. This example does not prove that Simpson is always more accurate for every integrand.')]
MAPLE=[heading('12.2.2 MAPLE commands “trapezoid” for trapezoidal rule and “Simpson” for Simpson’s rule and demonstrate through examples'),paragraph('The use of MAPLE commands trapezoid and simpson is illustrated below.'),heading('Example 7'),paragraph('Use MAPLE command to find approximate area ∫₀¹e^(1−x) dx on [0,1] by (a) trapezoidal rule and (b) Simpson rule.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('(a) The command below shows details of the trapezoidal rule:'),command('?trapezoid'),command('with(Student[Calculus1]):'),command('ApproximateInt(exp(-x), x=0..1, method=trapezoid, output=plot);'),figure('numerical322-trapezoid-output','Source MAPLE trapezoid output','Printed uniform approximation plot for e^(−x) over [0,1].'),paragraph('An approximation of ∫₀¹f(x)dx using trapezoid rule, where f(x)=e^(−x) and the partition is uniform. The approximate value is 0.6326472382. Number of subintervals used: 10.'),paragraph('(b) The command below shows details of Simpson rule:'),command('?simpson'),command('with(Student[Calculus1]):'),command('ApproximateInt(exp(-x), x=0..1, method=simpson, output=plot);'),figure('numerical322-simpson-output','Source MAPLE Simpson output','Printed uniform Simpson approximation plot for e^(−x) over [0,1].'),paragraph('An approximation of ∫₀¹f(x)dx using Simpson’s rule, where f(x)=e^(−x) and the partition is uniform. The approximate value is 0.6321205808. Number of subintervals used: 10.'),note('The stem integrand e^(1−x) differs from every worked command/output, which uses e^(−x). The exact values are e−1≈1.7182818285 for the stem and 1−e^(−1)≈0.6321205588 for the worked integrand. Standard composite trapezoid with 10 subintervals gives 0.6326472382, matching the print. Standard composite Simpson with 10 subintervals gives 0.6321209096; the printed 0.6321205808 matches 20 subintervals (10 two-interval panels). The printed count therefore mixes subinterval/panel conventions; no undocumented Maple default behavior is asserted. Original software plots and captions are retained; commands are not newly executed in SpaceBook.')]
